🎯 Attack Logic (The Hacker's Trick)
The Blueprint: The attacker delivers a weaponized PDF masquerading as a critical system update (Update.pdf)
via a targeted phishing email. The document contains no visible exploits or standard macros, rendering 
signature-based scanners ineffective.

The Mechanism: The attack exploits structural features of the PDF format, specifically embedding elements
like /OpenAction and /Launch. The moment the victim opens the document, these objects automatically trigger 
a chain reaction: executing highly obfuscated JavaScript and initiating native Windows PowerShell processes
in the background.

Evasion Technique: The payload utilizes multi-layered obfuscation to confuse static analysis tools:
JavaScript Layer: Enclosed in a heavily obfuscated structure running through the dangerous dynamic eval() 
function to hide its execution path.
PowerShell Layer: Hardcoded inside a raw object stream utilizing a combination of Base64 encoding and string 
reversal (Backward text) to break standard keyword detection.

Persistence Mechanism: The script binds multiple commands together into persistent variables (LoadCode) 
to establish a continuous connection to the attacker's server.

🕵️‍♂️ Investigation Process (How to Spot It)
Structural Assessment: Run specialized PDF indexers to identify risky object flags (/OpenAction, /Launch, /JS).
Object Cross-Referencing: Map specific object IDs to isolate structural payloads and view their raw data streams.
Deobfuscation & String Reversal: Extract encoded streams, decode them from Base64, and reverse the text order 
to read the underlying code.
Safe Dynamic Execution: Safe-test scripts by replacing active execution commands 
(like PowerShell's Invoke-Expression or JavaScript's eval()) with benign output strings (Write-Output/variables)
within sandboxed environments (Tio.run, W3Schools Tryit Editor) to extract cleartext Indicators of Compromise 
(IOCs).

